Vulkan RSX backend on Windows, and its engine path batched.

- The D3D12 backend has only the old live-draw path, which draws the scene into a supersampled target it never
  resolves (a black picture after the intro). The Vulkan backend runs the register-file draw engine, as Metal does,
  so it is built for Windows too with PS3RECOMP_RSX_VULKAN: vulkan-1.dll through LoadLibrary, SDL2 (static) for the
  window, glslang for the guest programs. D3D12 stays in the build as the fallback.
- Draws, uploads, clears, target creation, depth snapshots and the GPU resolve copy (vkCmdCopyImage) are recorded
  into one open command buffer; consecutive draws into the same framebuffer share a render pass. Vertices, indices,
  constants, staging and descriptor sets come from per-batch arenas. The batch is submitted and waited on only for a
  readback, a present, submit_and_wait, or before a framebuffer, pipeline or render target goes away.
- A present is one submission and one wait (the blit onto the display image and a centre-pixel copy in the frame's
  batch, the swapchain blit a second command buffer waiting for the acquire); the readback staging buffer is kept
  and grown.

